 "cells": [
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "source": "# 1.05 — Affine Geometry and Barycentric Coordinates\n\nTake the first step up the Erlangen ladder. An isometry was $x \\mapsto Ax + b$ with $A$ **orthogonal**. Drop that constraint and keep only invertibility:\n\n$$f(x) = Ax + b, \\qquad A \\in GL_n(\\mathbb R).$$\n\nThese are the **affine maps**, and they form the affine group $\\mathrm{Aff}(n)$, with $E(n)$ sitting inside it as the subgroup where $A^{\\mathsf T}A = I$. The group got bigger, so the invariants get fewer — and the interesting question is which survive.\n\n| Preserved by affine maps | Destroyed |\n|---|---|\n| collinearity, and lines → lines | length |\n| parallelism | angle |\n| ratios of lengths **along a line** | ratios of lengths in different directions |\n| midpoints, centroids | circles (they become ellipses) |\n| ratios of areas | area itself |\n| convexity | perpendicularity |\n\nThe middle row is the crucial one. \"Halfway along this segment\" is an affine notion; \"one metre along this segment\" is not. Anything you can say using only ratios along lines is affine geometry, and this notebook shows that a surprising amount of classical triangle geometry lives there — the concurrence of the medians, Ceva's theorem, Menelaus' theorem.\n\nThe right coordinate system for such statements is **barycentric**: describe a point by how much weight to place at each vertex of a reference triangle. Distances never appear, so every theorem written in these terms is automatically affine.",
   "metadata": {}
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "source": "## 1. What affine maps preserve\n\n**Lines go to lines.** A line is $\\{p + t d\\}$, and $f(p + td) = (Ap + b) + t(Ad)$ — another line, with direction $Ad$. Since $A$ is invertible, $Ad \\neq 0$.\n\n**Parallels stay parallel.** Two lines are parallel iff their directions are proportional, and $A$ preserves proportionality.\n\n**Ratios along a line are preserved.** If $r = (1-t)p + tq$ then\n\n$$f(r) = A((1-t)p + tq) + b = (1-t)(Ap + b) + t(Aq + b) = (1-t)f(p) + t f(q),$$\n\nusing $(1-t) + t = 1$. The same computation shows what an affine map really *is*: a map preserving **affine combinations** $\\sum \\lambda_i p_i$ with $\\sum \\lambda_i = 1$. That constraint on the weights is what makes the $b$ terms cancel, and it is the reason affine combinations — unlike linear ones — make sense without choosing an origin.\n\n**Ratios of areas are preserved.** Area scales by $|\\det A|$ everywhere, so any *ratio* of areas is unchanged. This is why \"the median divides the triangle into two equal halves\" is affine, while \"the area is 6\" is not.\n\n**Three points determine everything.** An affine map of the plane has 6 parameters, and prescribing the images of 3 non-collinear points imposes exactly 6 conditions. So there is a **unique** affine map taking any triangle to any other. Affinely speaking, all triangles are the same triangle — which is why an affine theorem about triangles need only be proved for the convenient one.",
   "metadata": {}
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "source": "load(\"numerics\")$\nload(\"ax-plots\")$\nload(\"linearalgebra\")$\nratprint : false$\n\ncvec(v) := transpose(matrix(v))$\npt(M)   := args(transpose(M))[1]$    /* column matrix → coordinate list */\n\n/* The unique affine map sending triangle (P1,P2,P3) to (Q1,Q2,Q3).\n   Solve A·Pi + b = Qi for the six unknowns. */\naffine_map(Ps, Qs) := block([A, b, eqs, sol],\n  A : matrix([a11, a12], [a21, a22]),\n  b : [b1, b2],\n  eqs : [],\n  for i : 1 thru 3 do block([img],\n    img : pt(A . cvec(Ps[i])) + b,\n    eqs : append(eqs, [img[1] = Qs[i][1], img[2] = Qs[i][2]]) ),\n  sol : solve(eqs, [a11, a12, a21, a22, b1, b2])[1],\n  [ subst(sol, A), subst(sol, b) ] )$\n\n/* Map the standard triangle to an arbitrary one */\nstd : [[0,0], [1,0], [0,1]]$\ntri : [[1,1], [4,2], [2,5]]$\nM   : affine_map(std, tri)$\nprint(\"A = \", M[1], \"   b = \", M[2])$\n\napply_aff(M, v) := pt(M[1] . cvec(v)) + M[2]$\nprint(\"images of the standard triangle: \",\n      map(lambda([v], apply_aff(M, v)), std), \"  = \", tri)$\n\n/* Area scales by |det A| — uniformly, so ratios of areas survive */\narea(P, Q, R) := abs( (Q[1]-P[1])*(R[2]-P[2]) - (R[1]-P[1])*(Q[2]-P[2]) )/2$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"area(std) = \", area(std[1], std[2], std[3]),\n      \"   area(tri) = \", area(tri[1], tri[2], tri[3]),\n      \"   |det A| = \", abs(determinant(M[1])))$",
   "metadata": {},
   "execution_count": 1,
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "A =  matrix([3,1],[1,4])    b =  [1,1]\nimages of the standard triangle:  [[1,1],[4,2],[2,5]]   =  [[1,1],[4,2],[2,5]]\narea(std) =  1/2    area(tri) =  11/2    |det A| =  11"
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "source": "## 2. Barycentric coordinates\n\nFix a triangle $ABC$ (non-degenerate). Every point $P$ of the plane can be written uniquely as an affine combination\n\n$$P = \\alpha A + \\beta B + \\gamma C, \\qquad \\alpha + \\beta + \\gamma = 1,$$\n\nand $(\\alpha, \\beta, \\gamma)$ are the **barycentric coordinates** of $P$. The name is mechanical: place masses $\\alpha, \\beta, \\gamma$ at the vertices and $P$ is the centre of mass — Möbius's original 1827 formulation.\n\nThree features make these the right coordinates for affine geometry:\n\n- **No origin, no metric.** The definition uses only affine combinations, so barycentric coordinates are *unchanged* by any affine map. A statement in barycentric coordinates is an affine statement, automatically.\n- **The vertices are $(1,0,0)$, $(0,1,0)$, $(0,0,1)$** — perfectly symmetric, unlike Cartesian coordinates which always privilege one corner.\n- **The coordinates are signed area ratios:**\n  $$\\alpha = \\frac{[PBC]}{[ABC]}, \\qquad \\beta = \\frac{[APC]}{[ABC]}, \\qquad \\gamma = \\frac{[ABP]}{[ABC]},$$\n  which is why they are affine — ratios of areas are preserved. Inside the triangle all three are positive; crossing the side $BC$ flips the sign of $\\alpha$.\n\nBecause only the ratios matter once we normalise, it is common to write **unnormalised** coordinates $(\\alpha : \\beta : \\gamma)$, scaling to sum to $1$ when needed. That colon is not an accident of notation: it is the same projective bracket we meet properly in 2.02, and barycentric coordinates are a first taste of homogeneous coordinates.",
   "metadata": {}
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "source": "/* A symbolic reference triangle — nothing below will depend on its shape */\nAv : [ax, ay]$  Bv : [bx, by]$  Cv : [cx, cy]$\n\n/* Barycentric coordinates of a point, by solving the defining equations */\nbary(P) := block([sol],\n  sol : solve([ α*Av[1] + β*Bv[1] + γ*Cv[1] = P[1],\n                α*Av[2] + β*Bv[2] + γ*Cv[2] = P[2],\n                α + β + γ = 1 ], [α, β, γ])[1],\n  ratsimp( subst(sol, [α, β, γ]) ) )$\n\n/* Reconstruct a point from barycentric coordinates */\nfrombary(w) := ratsimp( w[1]*Av + w[2]*Bv + w[3]*Cv )$\n\n/* Signed area, for the area-ratio interpretation */\nsarea(P, Q, R) := ((Q[1]-P[1])*(R[2]-P[2]) - (R[1]-P[1])*(Q[2]-P[2]))/2$\n\n/* The vertices, and the midpoint of BC */\nprint(\"bary(A)          = \", bary(Av))$\nprint(\"bary(B)          = \", bary(Bv))$\nprint(\"bary(midpoint BC)= \", bary((Bv + Cv)/2))$\n\n/* The coordinates really are signed area ratios */\nP0 : frombary([1/6, 1/3, 1/2])$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"P with weights (1/6, 1/3, 1/2):\")$\nprint(\"  [PBC]/[ABC] = \", ratsimp( sarea(P0, Bv, Cv) / sarea(Av, Bv, Cv) ))$\nprint(\"  [APC]/[ABC] = \", ratsimp( sarea(Av, P0, Cv) / sarea(Av, Bv, Cv) ))$\nprint(\"  [ABP]/[ABC] = \", ratsimp( sarea(Av, Bv, P0) / sarea(Av, Bv, Cv) ))$",
   "metadata": {},
   "execution_count": 2,
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "bary(A)          =  [1,0,0]\nbary(B)          =  [0,1,0]\nbary(midpoint BC)=  [0,1/2,1/2]\nP with weights (1/6, 1/3, 1/2):\n  [PBC]/[ABC] =  1/6\n  [APC]/[ABC] =  1/3\n  [ABP]/[ABC] =  1/2"
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "source": "## 3. Cevians: medians, Ceva and Menelaus\n\nA **cevian** is a segment from a vertex to a point on the opposite side. Barycentric coordinates make cevian geometry almost mechanical, because both of the operations we need are determinants:\n\n- three points $(\\alpha_i : \\beta_i : \\gamma_i)$ are **collinear** iff $\\det = 0$;\n- the line through two points has coefficient vector given by their **cross product**, and three lines are **concurrent** iff the determinant of their coefficient vectors vanishes.\n\n(Both facts are really projective, and 2.02–2.03 prove them properly. Here we simply use them.)\n\nParametrise the three side points, each running from one vertex to the next:\n\n$$D = (0 : 1-t : t) \\text{ on } BC, \\qquad E = (u : 0 : 1-u) \\text{ on } CA, \\qquad F = (1-v : v : 0) \\text{ on } AB,$$\n\nso that the signed ratios are\n\n$$\\frac{BD}{DC} = \\frac{t}{1-t}, \\qquad \\frac{CE}{EA} = \\frac{u}{1-u}, \\qquad \\frac{AF}{FB} = \\frac{v}{1-v}.$$\n\nNow let the determinants speak.",
   "metadata": {}
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "source": "/* Work entirely in barycentric triples */\ncross3(a, b) := [ a[2]*b[3] - a[3]*b[2],\n                  a[3]*b[1] - a[1]*b[3],\n                  a[1]*b[2] - a[2]*b[1] ]$\ndet3(a, b, c) := determinant(matrix(a, b, c))$\n\nAb : [1,0,0]$  Bb : [0,1,0]$  Cb : [0,0,1]$\n\nD : [0, 1-t, t]$\nE : [u, 0, 1-u]$\nF : [1-v, v, 0]$\n\n/* --- The medians: t = u = v = 1/2 --- */\nDm : subst(t = 1/2, D)$  Em : subst(u = 1/2, E)$  Fm : subst(v = 1/2, F)$\nmed_A : cross3(Ab, Dm)$  med_B : cross3(Bb, Em)$  med_C : cross3(Cb, Fm)$\nprint(\"median from A, as a line: \", med_A)$\nprint(\"concurrency determinant : \", det3(med_A, med_B, med_C), \"  (zero ⟹ concurrent)\")$\n\n/* The common point is the centroid (1:1:1) */\nG : cross3(med_A, med_B)$\nprint(\"intersection of two medians: \", G, \"  → normalised \", G/(G[1]+G[2]+G[3]))$\nprint(\"does it lie on the third median?  \", ratsimp(G . med_C))$\nprint(\"centroid in Cartesian coordinates: \", frombary([1/3,1/3,1/3]),\n      \"   = (A+B+C)/3 ✓\")$\n\n/* --- Ceva's theorem --- */\nlineAD : cross3(Ab, D)$  lineBE : cross3(Bb, E)$  lineCF : cross3(Cb, F)$\nceva : expand( det3(lineAD, lineBE, lineCF) )$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"Ceva determinant                = \", ceva)$\nprint(\"−[ tuv − (1−t)(1−u)(1−v) ]      = \", expand( -(t*u*v - (1-t)*(1-u)*(1-v)) ))$\nprint(\"identical, so the cevians concur ⟺ tuv = (1−t)(1−u)(1−v),\")$\nprint(\"which is exactly  (BD/DC)(CE/EA)(AF/FB) = 1.\")$\n\n/* --- Menelaus' theorem: D, E, F collinear --- */\nmen : expand( det3(D, E, F) )$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"Menelaus determinant            = \", men)$\nprint(\"  tuv + (1−t)(1−u)(1−v)         = \", expand( t*u*v + (1-t)*(1-u)*(1-v) ))$\nprint(\"so D, E, F are collinear ⟺ tuv = −(1−t)(1−u)(1−v),\")$\nprint(\"i.e. the same product equals −1. The sign is the whole difference\")$\nprint(\"between the two theorems: concurrent cevians vs a transversal line.\")$",
   "metadata": {},
   "execution_count": 4,
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "median from A, as a line:  [0,-(1/2),1/2]\nconcurrency determinant :  0   (zero ⟹ concurrent)\nintersection of two medians:  [1/4,1/4,1/4]   → normalised  [1/3,1/3,1/3]\ndoes it lie on the third median?   0\ncentroid in Cartesian coordinates:  [(cx+bx+ax)/3,(cy+by+ay)/3]\n                                      = (A+B+C)/3 ✓\nCeva determinant                =  -(2*t*u*v)+u*v+t*v-v+t*u-u-t+1\n−[ tuv − (1−t)(1−u)(1−v) ]      =  -(2*t*u*v)+u*v+t*v-v+t*u-u-t+1\nidentical, so the cevians concur ⟺ tuv = (1−t)(1−u)(1−v),\nwhich is exactly  (BD/DC)(CE/EA)(AF/FB) = 1.\nMenelaus determinant            =  u*v+t*v-v+t*u-u-t+1\n  tuv + (1−t)(1−u)(1−v)         =  u*v+t*v-v+t*u-u-t+1\nso D, E, F are collinear ⟺ tuv = −(1−t)(1−u)(1−v),\ni.e. the same product equals −1. The sign is the whole difference\nbetween the two theorems: concurrent cevians vs a transversal line."
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "source": "## 4. What is lost: circles become ellipses\n\nThe clearest way to feel the difference between $E(2)$ and $\\mathrm{Aff}(2)$ is to watch a circle. Under an isometry a circle stays a circle. Under a general affine map it becomes an **ellipse** — and every ellipse arises this way, since any positive-definite quadratic form can be brought to $x^2 + y^2$ by a linear change of variables.\n\nSo in affine geometry there is only one conic of each type: all ellipses are affinely equivalent, as are all parabolas, and all hyperbolas. Three classes, distinguished by how the curve meets the line at infinity — which is a projective statement, and the reason 2.04 can go one better and make *all* non-degenerate conics equivalent.\n\nNote what does survive. The centre of the ellipse is the image of the centre of the circle (centres are affine — they are midpoints of chords). Conjugate diameters are the images of perpendicular diameters, and the parallelogram they span has constant area. What is lost is precisely the notion of *which* diameters are perpendicular, since perpendicularity is a metric idea and the metric is gone.",
   "metadata": {}
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "source": "/* The affine map from before: A = [[3,1],[1,4]], b = (1,1) */\nAa : M[1]$  ba : M[2]$\n\n/* The unit circle and its image; the standard triangle and its image */\ncirc  : makelist( [cos(2*π*k/120), sin(2*π*k/120)], k, 0, 120)$\nellip : map(lambda([v], float(pt(Aa . cvec(v)) + ba)), circ)$\n\ntrip  : append(std, [std[1]])$\ntrip2 : map(lambda([v], float(pt(Aa . cvec(v)) + ba)), trip)$\n\nas_curve(pts) := [discrete, map(first, float(pts)), map(second, float(pts))]$\n\n/* Two perpendicular diameters of the circle, and their (non-perpendicular) images */\ndiam1 : [[-1,0],[1,0]]$  diam2 : [[0,-1],[0,1]]$\nd1i : map(lambda([v], float(pt(Aa . cvec(v)) + ba)), diam1)$\nd2i : map(lambda([v], float(pt(Aa . cvec(v)) + ba)), diam2)$\n\nprint(\"the images of two perpendicular diameters meet at angle \",\n      float( acos( ((d1i[2]-d1i[1]) . (d2i[2]-d2i[1])) /\n                   (sqrt((d1i[2]-d1i[1]) . (d1i[2]-d1i[1])) *\n                    sqrt((d2i[2]-d2i[1]) . (d2i[2]-d2i[1]))) ) * 180/π ),\n      \"° — no longer a right angle\")$\n\nplot2d( [as_curve(circ), as_curve(trip), as_curve(ellip), as_curve(trip2),\n         as_curve(d1i), as_curve(d2i)],\n        [x, -3, 8], [y, -3, 8],\n        [legend, false], [same_xy, true],\n        [title, \"an affine map: circle → ellipse, triangle → triangle\"])$",
   "metadata": {},
   "execution_count": 5,
   "outputs": [
