   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "source": "## Summary\n\nEnlarging the group from $E(2)$ to $\\mathrm{Aff}(2)$ cost us length, angle and circles, and left us with collinearity, parallelism, ratios along lines, and ratios of areas. That residue is enough for a real body of theorems.\n\n**Barycentric coordinates** are the natural language for it. A point is $(\\alpha : \\beta : \\gamma)$ relative to a reference triangle, the coordinates are signed area ratios, and — because the definition never mentions distance — any statement expressed in them is affine-invariant for free.\n\nThree results, all from determinants:\n\n| Statement | Condition |\n|---|---|\n| medians concurrent | at the centroid $(1:1:1)$ |\n| **Ceva** — cevians concurrent | $\\dfrac{BD}{DC}\\cdot\\dfrac{CE}{EA}\\cdot\\dfrac{AF}{FB} = +1$ |\n| **Menelaus** — $D, E, F$ collinear | the same product $= -1$ |\n\nThat Ceva and Menelaus differ only in a sign is not a coincidence to be memorised. Both are the vanishing of a $3\\times3$ determinant — one built from the lines, one from the points — and the sign difference is the difference between three lines meeting in a point and three points lying on a line. That is **duality**, and 2.02 makes it a theorem rather than an observation.\n\n## Looking up the ladder\n\nWe have now climbed one rung, and the same move works again:\n\n| Group | Fixes | Sample invariant |\n|---|---|---|\n| $E(2)$ — isometries | distances | length, angle |\n| $\\mathrm{Aff}(2)$ — affine maps | the line at infinity | ratio along a line, area ratio |\n| $PGL(3)$ — projective maps | nothing | the cross-ratio |\n\nAffine geometry is exactly projective geometry with one line singled out as \"infinity\" — the line that parallel lines meet on. Give up that last piece of structure and ratios along a line die too, but a subtler invariant of *four* collinear points survives. That is where Stage 2 begins.\n\n## Next\n\n**1.06 — Conics and Quadrics** stays in the Euclidean world for one more notebook and classifies the quadratic curves and surfaces by diagonalising their quadratic forms — the first real payoff for the eigenvalue theory of [linalg-journey](https://github.com/cmsd2/linalg-journey).",
   "metadata": {}
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "source": "## References\n\n- Berger, M. (1987) *Geometry I*. Berlin: Springer. Chapters 2–3 for the affine group and its invariants, done thoroughly.\n- Coxeter, H.S.M. (1969) *Introduction to Geometry*. 2nd edn. New York: Wiley. Chapter 13 for affine geometry, Chapter 11 for Ceva and Menelaus.\n- Artin, E. (1957) *Geometric Algebra*. New York: Interscience. The algebraic view of affine and projective geometry.\n\nBarycentric coordinates are due to Möbius, A.F. (1827) *Der barycentrische Calcul*. Leipzig: Barth.\n- ★ Stillwell, J. (2005) *The Four Pillars of Geometry*. Undergraduate Texts in Mathematics. New York: Springer. doi:10.1007/0-387-29052-4. Chapters 5–6 for affine and projective coordinates.\n- Samuel, P. (1988) *Projective Geometry*. Undergraduate Texts in Mathematics. New York: Springer. ISBN 978-0-387-96752-3. For the projective sequel in 2.02.\n\n---\n*Marked ★ = in the local indexed library (`maths` collection); the rest are standard references given from knowledge, not consulted here. Every code cell in this notebook was executed and its output is the real result; the prose is written from knowledge and checked against these sources where they cover the material.*",
   "metadata": {}
  }
 ]
}